    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-104786",
      "knowledge_point_id": "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/7c4260d8cdf3557b6c6f762bf83862297812368d521104ba27995778a0cb0ff7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+2g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)+2{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/4ced2c562500bfa0d352d4552f38f47b5caab9bdc68db5bc3dbfca43799a0f8f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{0-(-4)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)+2{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1+2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
