    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-104803",
      "knowledge_point_id": "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/ec9faab92da8de318d78e548adc35e8df20083816f8bafdacf0cfbbd497e084c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule and the addition rule for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=0$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)-{f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
