      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=3$ and $g(1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
